  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="countries">
    <h2 id="countries">35 countries, two data sources</h2>
    <p>In <strong>Japan, the United States, Australia, New Zealand, Argentina and Chile</strong>, Respira uses the Google Pollen API, updated daily for wherever you are: Japanese cedar (sugi), Japanese cypress (hinoki) and grass in Japan, with the previous day's counts measured by the Ministry of the Environment alongside in season; eleven species in the US; grass and cypress pine in Australia; grass in the other three. Across <strong>Europe</strong>, it uses the Copernicus CAMS model: Spain, Portugal, France, Italy, Germany, Ireland and the United Kingdom, Central Europe (Austria, Switzerland, the Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg, Poland, Czechia, Slovakia, Hungary, Slovenia), the Nordics and Baltics (Sweden, Norway, Denmark, Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania) and south-east Europe (Greece, Croatia, Romania, Bulgaria, Serbia). The national monitoring networks of Spain, Italy and Germany add measured readings where they publish them.</p>
    <p>Which species you are offered depends on where you are, because the honest answer differs: ragweed, oak and mountain cedar matter in the United States, grass and cypress pine drive hay fever in Australia, birch dominates the Nordic spring, ragweed hits Hungary, Serbia and Romania in late summer, and olive and pellitory belong to the Mediterranean. Respira never shows a level for a species its source does not report there.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cities">
    <h2 id="cities">Pollen by city</h2>
    <p>Every US metro has a different pollen year: ragweed defines late summer in Minneapolis, mountain cedar defines December in Austin, and alder starts in February in Seattle before anywhere else has begun.</p>
    <ul class="link-grid">
      {%- for c in usCities | slice(1) | first %}{% if loop.index <= 12 %}
      <li><a href="/en/pollen-{{ c.slug }}">{{ c.name }}, {{ c.abbr }}</a></li>
      {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/en/pollen-map-usa">All 50 cities</a></p>
    <p>In the UK and Ireland, each city page has today's level by type from the Copernicus CAMS model and its own month-by-month calendar: alder from February in London, birch in April, grass from June.</p>
    <ul class="link-grid">
      {%- for c in ukCities %}{% if loop.index <= 8 %}
      <li><a href="{{ c.url }}">{{ c.name }}</a></li>
      {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/en/pollen-map-uk-ireland">All UK and Ireland cities</a></p>
    <p>In Australia, grass pollen runs from October to January across the south and longer from Brisbane north: <a href="/en/pollen-australia">the Australian grass and cypress pine seasons, and the 20 cities on the map</a>.</p>
    <p><a href="/en/pollen-calendar">Make a pollen calendar for your city and your allergies</a> · <a href="/en/pollen-food-allergy">Foods linked to your pollen allergy</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="allergens">
    <h2 id="allergens">Allergens in the United States</h2>
    <ul class="link-grid">
      {%- for info in usPollenInfoList %}
      <li><a href="/en/allergy-{{ info.slug }}">{{ info.h1 | replace(" allergy", "") | replace(" pollen", "") }}</a></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What the app does</h2>
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Today's levels, by species</h3>
        <p class="text-secondary">Only the allergens you have selected, only where they are actually reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Map and forecast</h3>
        <p class="text-secondary">The level in each city at a glance, and how the next days look where you are.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Air quality on the right scale</h3>
        <p class="text-secondary">The EPA index in the United States, the European index elsewhere &mdash; 49 is "Moderate" on one and "Good" on the other, so showing the wrong one would be worse than showing none.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Seasonal calendar</h3>
        <p class="text-secondary">When each pollen starts, peaks and ends in your region.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Diary and reminders</h3>
        <p class="text-secondary">Symptoms and medication day by day, and, if you turn it on, a heads-up the evening before when tomorrow is high for your allergens.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Widget</h3>
        <p class="text-secondary">Today's level on your home screen, without opening the app.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature pro">
        <h3>With Respira Pro</h3>
        <p class="text-secondary">Alerts the morning your allergen crosses the threshold you set, in any of your saved places; a trip planner with the destination's forecast; a PDF report for your allergist; and no ads.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="reviews">
    <h2 id="reviews">What people say on the App Store</h2>
    {{ m.testimonials(appStoreReviews.quotes.en, REVIEWS_T) }}
    <p class="small text-secondary" style="margin-block-start: var(--sp-4)">Real reviews, copied word for word from the App Store, without the reviewer's name.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="who">
    <h2 id="who">Who it's for</h2>
    <ul class="audience">
      <li><strong>People with hay fever</strong> who want to know what's in the air before going out, opening the windows or hanging the laundry outside.</li>
      <li><strong>Anyone seeing an allergist</strong> who wants their symptoms and medication logged day by day, next to that day's pollen.</li>
      <li><strong>People who travel or move</strong>: the same app works in 35 countries, each with its own data source.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="honest">
    <h2 id="honest">When we have no data, we say so</h2>
    <p>Pollen models have gaps &mdash; a species with no coverage, a source that did not answer, a cell outside the model's domain. Respira shows "no reading" for those rather than a zero. "No pollen" is a measurement; "we have no data" is not, and the two should never look the same on a screen you are using to decide whether to go outside.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    {{ m.ctaApp("Download Respira free", "Pollen levels for where you are, a multi-day forecast, air quality and a symptom diary. Free, for iPhone and iPad.", i18nWeb.en) }}
  </section>

  <p class="fine-print">Respira is an informational tool. It does not replace diagnosis or advice from a medical professional.</p>
</main>

